import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.C1oW5ckO.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Trusted publishing for npm tools","description":"","frontmatter":{"navigation":{"category":"CI and releases"},"editLink":"https://github.com/filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins/edit/master/docs/npm-trusted-publishing.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/npm-trusted-publishing.md","filePath":"docs/npm-trusted-publishing.md","lastUpdated":1792014225000}`),a={name:`guides/npm-trusted-publishing.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="trusted-publishing-for-npm-tools" tabindex="-1">Trusted publishing for npm tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#trusted-publishing-for-npm-tools" aria-label="Permalink to “Trusted publishing for npm tools”">​</a></h1><p>The release workflow publishes the public tools with npm trusted publishing. npm authenticates the GitHub Actions workflow through OpenID Connect (OIDC); no npm write token is stored in GitHub secrets.</p><h2 id="prerequisites" tabindex="-1">Prerequisites <a class="header-anchor" href="#prerequisites" aria-label="Permalink to “Prerequisites”">​</a></h2><p>Configure a trusted publisher only after its npm package exists. The metadata-only <code>0.0.0</code> placeholder satisfies this requirement but contains no working package code. Because the new relationship must be validated by a successful publish within two days, wait until the real Release Please release is ready to merge before creating it. npm also requires package write access, account-level two-factor authentication, and npm CLI <code>11.15.0</code> or newer for the <code>npm trust</code> command. Check the CLI with <code>npm --version</code>; update it with <code>npm install --global npm@^11.15.0</code> if needed.</p><p>For new trusted publishers, npm automatically allows <code>npm stage publish</code>. The <code>--allow-publish</code> flag also allows direct <code>npm publish</code>, which this repository&#39;s release workflow uses. See npm&#39;s current <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers/#configuring-trusted-publishing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">trusted publisher permissions</a>.</p><p>Run direct npm commands from outside the repository root, such as <code>/tmp</code>, to avoid the root <code>devEngines</code> package-manager requirement.</p><p>The first functional version is published by the Release Please workflow. See <a href="./npm-publishing">Publishing public npm tools</a> for the placeholder and release sequence.</p><h2 id="configure-the-trusted-publisher" tabindex="-1">Configure the trusted publisher <a class="header-anchor" href="#configure-the-trusted-publisher" aria-label="Permalink to “Configure the trusted publisher”">​</a></h2><p>For each package, add a GitHub Actions trusted publisher with these values:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>npm setting</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>GitHub organization or user</td><td><code>filiphsps</code></td></tr><tr><td>GitHub repository</td><td><code>pumpkin-plugins</code></td></tr><tr><td>Workflow filename</td><td><code>ci.yml</code></td></tr><tr><td>Environment name</td><td>Leave blank</td></tr><tr><td>Allowed actions</td><td><code>npm stage publish</code> (automatic) and direct <code>npm publish</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Run this command after substituting the package name and checking its current relationships below. The registry readback should identify <code>ci.yml</code>, <code>filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins</code>, and both publish permissions (<code>createPackage</code> and <code>createStagedPackage</code>):</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> trust</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> github</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">package-nam</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --file</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ci.yml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --repo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --allow-publish</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --yes</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Check the package&#39;s existing relationships first with <code>npm trust list &lt;package&gt;</code>. If the exact <code>ci.yml</code> relationship is already present, do not add it again. If no relationship exists, run the command above. Preserve every unrelated relationship. The current npm docs disagree: the <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers/#managing-trusted-publisher-configurations" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">trusted-publishers guide says a package can have up to 10 relationships</a>, while the <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v11/commands/npm-trust/#provider-options" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>npm trust</code> CLI reference says only one configuration per package is supported</a>. If any other relationship is listed, or the output does not make replacement behavior clear, stop before running the <code>--yes</code> command and resolve the conflict through a supported npm procedure. Do not revoke an unrelated entry or work around the limit. Do not revoke an existing relationship just to rerun setup.</p><p>The repository uses a reusable workflow: <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code> calls <code>.github/workflows/release.yml</code>. npm matches the caller&#39;s workflow filename, so configure <code>ci.yml</code>, not <code>release.yml</code>. OIDC requires <code>id-token: write</code> in both the caller&#39;s <code>release</code> job and the reusable workflow&#39;s <code>publish-tools</code> job. The publish job is gated by Release Please&#39;s <code>tool_paths_released</code> output. A GitHub Actions npm write token is not needed.</p><h2 id="validate-the-relationship-within-two-days" tabindex="-1">Validate the relationship within two days <a class="header-anchor" href="#validate-the-relationship-within-two-days" aria-label="Permalink to “Validate the relationship within two days”">​</a></h2><p>npm requires a newly created trusted-publisher relationship to complete its first successful OIDC publish within two days. Configure trust shortly before the real Release Please release is ready, merge that release PR promptly, then confirm the release succeeds and verify the version on npm. Publishing or retrying the already published <code>0.0.0</code> placeholder does not validate the trusted publisher.</p><p>If the relationship expires before a successful OIDC publish, list the package relationships and identify the expired <code>ci.yml</code> relationship by its identity and exact ID. Only revoke that expired relationship; preserve all unrelated entries. If the listed entries do not identify it unambiguously, stop before revoking anything:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> trust</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> list</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">packag</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Revoke that expired relationship, then create it again using the command above and complete a new-version OIDC publish within two days:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> trust</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> revoke</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">packag</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --id</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">expired-trust-i</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">d</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Do not remove unrelated relationships. A trusted-publisher configuration cannot be edited in place; after expiry or an identity error, revoke only the exact expired or incorrect relationship, then recreate it. The new relationship must complete a successful OIDC publish within two days.</p><h2 id="runtime-and-provenance" tabindex="-1">Runtime and provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#runtime-and-provenance" aria-label="Permalink to “Runtime and provenance”">​</a></h2><p>npm CLI <code>11.15.0</code> or newer is required to run the <code>npm trust</code> setup command. For the automated trusted-publish operation, npm CLI <code>11.5.1</code> or newer and Node <code>22.14.0</code> or newer are required. The repository&#39;s publish workflow uses Node <code>24.21.0</code> and checks the npm minimum before publishing. The workflow publishes from GitHub-hosted runners in this public GitHub repository.</p><p>npm automatically generates provenance for public packages published through trusted publishing from a public repository. The workflow also passes <code>--provenance</code> explicitly when publishing, so the release command records that requirement directly. Provenance links a package version to its source and publishing workflow; it does not by itself guarantee that the package is safe. See npm&#39;s <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/trusted-publishers/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">trusted publishing documentation</a>, <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v11/commands/npm-trust/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>npm trust</code> reference</a>, and <a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/generating-provenance-statements/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">provenance guide</a>.</p>`,24)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};